@@ -0,0 +1,57 @@
+# ECON-02B2d15 — Production-Line Shutdown and NPC Inventory Evacuation
+
+**Date:** 2026-11-01  
+**Project:** Single Galaxy (original game, not EVEmu)  
+**Source baseline:** `SingleGalaxy_ECON02B2d14_Machine_Dismantling_Source_Tests_2026-11-01.zip` (613-file source archive)  
+**Status:** Implemented and Linux-tested; not committed to GitHub; Windows GUI acceptance open.
+
+## Summary
+
+A colony administrator can **explicitly shut down or resume** the existing two-stage (primary processor + finisher) workshop. Shutdown rejects new feed or inbound stock and prevents new production batches, but previously started work-in-progress finishes and remains accounted for. The NPC scheduler releases workshop operators once WIP drains. The shutdown flag is durable across process and SQLite restarts.
+
+An administrator can then assign one **named on-site habitat resident** to evacuate remaining workshop inventory to the physically installed original storage bin. The worker walks through the same provisional colony-grid collision/navigation layer as construction crews; each trip carries at most **20 kg** of one of four stock classes: aluminum, panels, frames, or scrap. An authoritative `PickupWorkshopGoods` command subtracts real workshop inventory and adds it to the worker's persistent cargo; `DepositWorkshopGoods` moves that cargo into the actual storage-bin inventory only after the worker reaches the bin. `ReturnWorkshopGoods` handles cancellation by walking back to the workshop. No warehouse-to-warehouse teleportation or resource fabrication is permitted.
+
+These operations run in the **one authoritative C++ colony simulation**, through conversation/session-bound native host controls and a localhost HTTP bridge with token/origin/site checks. SQLite stores job intent and grid navigation; the authoritative snapshot and audited command/resource journal record physical custody. Lost or forged resource receipts cause validation failure on recovery.
+
+## Authoritative commands and durable state
+
+- `SetWorkshopShutdown`: owner-only; amount 1=stop/drain, 0=resume. Blocks `TransferWorkshopFeed`, inbound `TransferWorkshopStack`, incoming storage transfers and automatic conveyor feeds. Existing WIP can complete.
+- `PickupWorkshopGoods`: owner-only authorized named resident, valid outpost and stock type, 1–20 kg per action; requires shutdown and drained WIP. Worker has a strict aggregate 20 kg carrying capacity.
+- `DepositWorkshopGoods`: requires actual carried cargo and available physical capacity in the original built storage bin.
+- `ReturnWorkshopGoods`: cancellation returns actual carried stock to the workshop; no refund without cargo.
+- Snapshot **v39** records `shutdown_requested` and site/NPC cargo arrays; compatible readers accept earlier v35–v38 world states without requiring prior data to have the new fields. Event/journal command validation recognizes the new action IDs.
+- Audit records `npc_workshop_inventory_transfer_kg` with fully derived source/destination endpoint strings and an exact-match event amount/tick/command. Forging a receipt endpoint is detected during SQLite reopen.
+- `sg_line_evacuation_jobs` is a non-destructive SQLite schema addition. Existing d14 line, dismantling, and other NPC records are retained.
+
+## Assignment and custody safeguards
+
+The host checks a real on-site resident identity, current habitat and employment reservations, placed storage and workshop equipment, shutdown state, no unfinished work-in-progress, no operators, and no competing active evacuation crew. NPCs assigned to evacuation cannot also be assigned to building, warehouse hauling/salvage, machine dismantling or autonomous building-staff selection. A queued cancellation does not destroy cargo; the worker returns it through the world command. Route failures are reported and do not conjure inventory at the destination.
+
+## Verified results on Linux
+
+| Check | Result |
+| --- | --- |
+| Complete CMake/Ninja C++ build | PASS |
+| CTest test indices 1–70 | **70/70 PASS** |
+| CTest test indices 71–105 | **35/35 PASS** |
+| JavaScript (`node --test tests/*.js`) | **93/93 PASS** |
+| Offline Chromium (`colony_crew_browser_smoke.py`) | PASS |
+| Offline Chromium (`colony_structures_browser_smoke.py`) | PASS |
+| Native d15 simulation + SQLite restart | PASS |
+| d15 real localhost authenticated HTTP + mid-haul process restart | PASS |
+| Forged workshop material-ledger receipt | Rejected (PASS) |
+
+The native test demonstrates: unauthorized shutdown refused, existing WIP drain, operator release, shutdown persistence, blocked inbound inputs, mass conservation, physical inventory pickup, cancellation during cargo transit, actual cargo return, re-queuing, restart while carrying, subsequent physical delivery to storage, and resumed production only after express request. The HTTP test verifies forged tokens, foreign origins, wrong site, malformed requests, NPC pickup and restart recovery.
+
+## Boundaries and known remaining work
+
+1. Current scope is the **original two-stage workshop and original built local storage bin**. Generic multi-machine line-graph shutdown, other storage nodes and cross-colony hauling are not yet implemented.
+2. This milestone does **not** automatically disconnect conveyor lines or schedule d14 machine teardown. Those operations remain separately authorized; shutdown/evacuation make them safer to initiate.
+3. The route model is the existing **provisional local 2D grid**. It is not full 3D NPC navigation, animated carrying, vehicle-based transport, or physical conveyor item-by-item simulation.
+4. Existing storage volume and carrying caps remain enforced. If the original bin is full or routes are blocked, the crew reports the constraint and waits with physical cargo; the host does not silently route to an alternative warehouse.
+5. Windows compilation and independent Windows GUI acceptance remain open. Linux local browser tests use mocked UI transport; a separate live HTTP test checks the real native host.
+6. GitHub remains the source repository / archival destination, not the development environment, and has **not** been updated by this milestone.
+
+## Next gate
+
+**ECON-02B2d16 — Dependency-ordered line retirement:** drain WIP, evacuate multiple storage destinations, remove conveyor links with NPC labor, then schedule machine teardown in topological order; verify locks, rollback, restart recovery and no loss of work or materials. Keep explicit player authorization and manage-absence policies separate from autonomous administrative proposals.
